  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nTemperatures will warm back up into the 90s today and Wednesday as \nthe upper ridging builds back eastward with 850 mb temperatures of \n20-24C. Tomorrow, moisture transport will increase ahead of the cold \nfront bringing soupy dewpoints in the 70s into the central part of \nthe state. There is a 50-80% chance of heat indices greater than 95 \ndegrees south of I-70 Wednesday afternoon, from the HREF. There \ncould be some limitations with how hot it could get on Wednesday \nwith the cloud cover from convective debris across the state and how \nfast the front may move through. For areas north of the IL River \nValley, today could be the hottest day out of the two.\n\nThe cold front will move through tomorrow into tomorrow night as the \nridge gets broken down by the upper low moving over Canada. It will \nclear out the sweltering mid to upper 90 temperatures, leaving highs \nin the low to mid 80s before building back to upper 80s to near 90 \nfor the weekend. The LREF suggests that there is a 50-70% chance of \nhigh temperatures cooler than 80 degrees by next Tuesday. Could \nAutumn be near?\n\nThis front will also bring the next chance for substantial rain and \nstrong-to-severe thunderstorms tomorrow into Thursday. The CAMs are \nshowing a line of showers & convection along the front entering the \nnorthwest around 10z Wednesday. The initial area of convection may \nwidely dry out during the day, leaving some scattered activity as \nthe front pushed southeast through the state. This scattered \nactivity could have strong to damaging winds and/or hail with them. \nLooks like by 00z Thursday, redevelopment will occur in the \nsoutheast in the evening as the LLJ kicks in, potentially training \nnear and south of I-70. This redevelopment could continue into \nThursday morning and may prove to be a flash flooding risk, rather \nthan severe. However, this area can likely take quite a bit of rain \nbefore flooding becomes a major concern. The 6 hour FFG for the \nsoutheast is 3-5 inches. QPF shows 1-3 inches of rainfall south of I-\n72, with the greatest amounts near and south of I-70, from 06z \nWednesday to 00z Friday. PWATs in that area are forecast to be 2.0-\n2.5 inches. \n\nWPC has maintained a D2 marginal risk ERO south of I-72, with a \nslight risk area just off to the east, including southwestern IL. \nSPC has expanded the D2 marginal risk for severe weather north to a \nsouthern Knox County to Livingston County line and south. Damaging \nwinds and hail are the most likely hazards with the area of storms. \n\nAdditional low-end chances exist for showers and thunderstorms \nthrough the weekend. Due to low predictability and model spread, \nbroad-brushed 20-40% PoPs remain in place from Friday night through \nearly next week.",
